{:edb/db-before-t 156 :edb/db-after-t 157 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533469 50 #inst "2026-10-11T01:13:04.445Z" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186064618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186061755 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186064618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396654 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186064618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186064618 1071 "For the function $f(x) = x - 2\\ln x$, defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2} \\le x \\le e$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2} + 2\\ln 2$\n2. The global minimum value is $e - 2$\n3. The global minimum value is $2 - 2\\ln 2$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186064618 1071 "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le e,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$\nII. The global minimum value is $e-2$\nIII. The global minimum value is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186064618 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=1-\\frac{2}{x},\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n1-\\frac{2}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},e$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈1.9 \\\\ f(2) & =2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2≈0.6 \\\\ f(e) & =e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=e-2≈0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{1}{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(2)=2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ so the global minimum is $2-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"I and III only.\"\n\n![](images/6c/6cc3ee476c8c5dc9e27051c990b184318147caffd2177b760074172fe5c667f6.png)" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396584 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186068702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396679 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186069374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067384 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186069374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396673 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186069374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186069374 1074 "The graph breaks at $x=-2$ and $x=0$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$ and $x=0.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396714 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396721 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186096638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396626 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186096638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396686 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186100924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396640 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115340 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396619 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114241 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186115889 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396667 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115889 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115889 1071 "![](images/8e/8e7daf95fadbe2262a09e8183f00d63075655f3b97dd363984c192ac3414ad17.png)\n\nConsider the function $y = f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" 13194139533469 17592186045585 false] [17592186115889 1071 "![](images/8e/8e7daf95fadbe2262a09e8183f00d63075655f3b97dd363984c192ac3414ad17.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186115889 1074 "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=-2$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=2$ the function has an infinite discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2$, $x=0$, and $x=2.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186119929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396591 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186119929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396700 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186119929 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396707 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186124480 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396693 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186147505 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396577 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186179729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396633 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186184202 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396598 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186184202 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396605 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186184202 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396612 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186184202 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396728 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186198276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396647 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396660 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396578 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1069 "q-103178" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1071 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$\nII. $xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$\nIII. $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1072 #uuid "8e4d2f85-2640-5963-a9fc-d023ea35768b" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396577 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-1\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\ne \\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ does *not* give an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$  gives an exact equation.\n\nHence, the correct answer is ''III only''." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396579 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396580 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396581 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396582 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396583 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1077 "selection" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396579 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396578 1080 #uuid "c0ee641f-c56c-5e39-8179-c1c054897293" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396579 1081 "ÍII only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396579 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396579 1084 #uuid "65cb7b3b-ca02-57e0-a31c-2311ebcd6860" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396580 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396580 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396580 1084 #uuid "21a7e756-add5-5d08-a0b6-865f25787d6d" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396581 1081 "I only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396581 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396581 1084 #uuid "0417384c-c1ac-529e-bf13-223231a54322" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396582 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396582 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396582 1084 #uuid "4737d57e-814c-5ac7-acfb-48ce13b5a3e9" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396583 1081 "II only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396583 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396583 1084 #uuid "95ce12e4-12c0-5c3c-9bc0-e72604c4d3e6" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396585 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1069 "q-104025" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1071 "![](images/2d/2d342689f9e4a213ac5407ccc132868b51ba218afabda1a871e7ca7b02345a17.png)\n\nWhat are the points of discontinuity of the function plotted above?" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1072 #uuid "c6685bf3-2db8-58de-88bd-a80799c06e2f" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396584 1074 "The graph breaks at $x=-1$ and $x=2,$ meaning that we can't draw the plot without taking our pencil off of the paper when passing through these points.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-1$ and $x=2.$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396586 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396587 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396588 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396589 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396590 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1077 "selection" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396587 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396585 1080 #uuid "53daf543-087e-50bb-928d-d5b44cae5b1c" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396586 1081 "$x=2$ only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396586 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396586 1084 #uuid "54ba84a1-3d25-5cd1-98bf-f731c2622e1d" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396587 1081 "$x=-1$ and $x=2$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396587 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396587 1084 #uuid "da75cf17-b00a-5ed0-97c9-6265dae86079" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396588 1081 "$x=1$ and $x=2$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396588 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396588 1084 #uuid "77df7b4b-c87e-5e29-808b-f38574caacd1" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396589 1081 "$x=-2$ and $x=1$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396589 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396589 1084 #uuid "8c127ea3-d160-5664-896d-8df951530b12" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396590 1081 "$x=-1$ only" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396590 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396590 1084 #uuid "d02a6f82-debb-558b-a876-dc02aab50551" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396592 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1069 "q-105291" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1071 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1072 #uuid "faa7ddb4-5b88-5e56-a53e-35fdb9d2ef93" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396591 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396593 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396594 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396595 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396596 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396597 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1077 "selection" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396596 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396592 1080 #uuid "a01010bc-5e11-583a-98fc-7b20994c0fc5" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396593 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396593 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396593 1084 #uuid "e5f3b240-c702-580d-9cdf-43123253f773" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396594 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396594 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396594 1084 #uuid "574dea89-ee9d-5556-a72b-c257c732699e" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396595 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396595 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396595 1084 #uuid "640a91d9-57f7-5e99-bc15-29e1456e8af0" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396596 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396596 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396596 1084 #uuid "2044901c-e971-534a-af35-fba5ec803324" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396597 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396597 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396597 1084 #uuid "3bf8a5fb-65f3-58d7-a288-3f0a87f9681a" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396599 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1069 "q-106929" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1071 "Let $Y$ be the set of all polynomials in the variable $x\\in R$ with coefficients in $R$ of degree exactly $2.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1072 #uuid "4c206776-99db-55ba-ae48-7c0ec7d6d597" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396598 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$Y$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **addition of polynomials in $Y$ is not closed.** *\n\nIndeed, let's consider, for example, the polynomials $p(x)={x}^{2}+x$ and $q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$ Then $p,q\\in Y,$ but \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)={x}^{2}+x+(-{x}^{2})=x\\notin Y\n$$\n\n because $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p+q)=1\\ne 2.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- $1\\cdot p=p$ for all $p\\in Y.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials satisfy the distributive laws. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,✓$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396599 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396600 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396599 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396601 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396599 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396602 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396599 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396603 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396599 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396604 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396599 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396599 1077 "selection" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396599 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396602 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396599 1080 #uuid "8e07246a-b3d7-5b8e-9074-824239bfbe46" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396600 1081 "addition of polynomials is not associative" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396600 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396600 1084 #uuid "0a58b77f-aa33-52d3-a1ab-4bbbe9ed2639" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396601 1081 "addition and scalar multiplication of polynomials do not satisfy the distributive laws" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396601 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396601 1084 #uuid "cb6dcbd0-7e6a-5124-8dfb-30e54362007c" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396602 1081 "addition of polynomials in $Y$ is not closed" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396602 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396602 1084 #uuid "3afd6545-9a30-5d6b-bce2-014c2d72383f" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396603 1081 "addition of polynomials is not commutative" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396603 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396603 1084 #uuid "8ab25e26-7932-539a-b22c-b438766b2222" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396604 1081 "$1\\cdot p\\ne p$ for some $p\\in Y$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396604 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396604 1084 #uuid "257d280c-e23a-51f0-8ab9-6c6a6d270300" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396606 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396605 1069 "q-106930" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396605 1071 "Let $L$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ of the form $f(x)=ax+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Complete the following sentence:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because $\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396605 1072 #uuid "15beea94-47dc-575a-be81-e2d3b2547e2f" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396605 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$L$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in L$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =2$ and $f(x)=x+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=2x+2.$ So, $\\alpha f(x)\\notin L$ because the constant term is not $1.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of real valued functions is commutative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in L.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition and scalar multiplication of real valued functions satisfy the distributive laws.\n$\\,✓$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396607 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396608 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396609 1319