469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396640 1071 "Which of the following sequences, defined for $n\\ge 1,$ converge?\n\n- ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}}$\n- ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}}$\n- ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}}$" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396640 1072 #uuid "b9bf7b26-4c35-5b3c-bd82-2290add25e67" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396640 1074 "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit of each sequence, we must evaluate them for $n\\to \\infty .$\n\nFor ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(3n)}{{e}^{2n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{\\sqrt{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{b}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFor ${c}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{e}^{n}},$ both the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{c}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"${a}_{n},$ ${b}_{n},$ and ${c}_{n}.$\"" 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396642 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396643 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396644 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396645 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396641 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396646 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17592186396641 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533469 17592186045585 true] [17